Contactor Control specification

1. With precharge circuit

The aim of the precharge circuit is to align the load voltage (usually an inverter) with the battery pack voltage by energizing the inverter from the battery.
By convention, the precharge circuit is on the positive side of the pack.
  
a. Closing Sequence:
The BMS when receiving a request to close the contactors, should check the following:
- Make sure the contactors are open.
- No critical fault (tbd).

In this configuration, the BMS should first close the negative contactor followed by the precharge contactor (at next tick) in a sequential order.
Once the link voltage is equal to the pack voltage (~5V difference), close the positive contactor and open the precharge contactor, in a sequential order by keeping the even a tick apart.
If the link voltage does not reach the pack voltage within Dt_prechgTimeOut (20s default), a timeout error should be thrown and the closing sequence should be stopped.

b. Opening Sequence:
When receiving the opening request, the BMS should wait for the current to be lower than minCurrentOpenThr (default 5A) before opening all the contactors simultaneously.
If the current has not been decreased after a certain amount of time Dt_loCurrentTimeout (default 30s) then open the contactors anyway.

c. Error opening:
When receiving a critical error from limit violation (cell voltage and temperature violation), open all the contactors immediately.
When receiving a mild error from limit violation, open all the contactors after a delay (10s - calibration). This will allow the power and current limits to ramp down to 0W/0A before opening the contactors.

2. Without precharge circuit (draft)
In some applications, the precharge circuit is located on the inverter side. The inverter then precharge itself to reach the pack voltage.

a. Closing Sequence:
When the BMS receives a request to close the contactors, it should check the following:
- Make sure the contactors are open.
- No critical fault (tbd).

Then the BMS shall close both the negative and positive to expose the terminal voltage of the battery to the inverter. The inverter will then align its own voltage internally.

b. Opening Sequence:
When receiving the the opening request, the BMS should wait for the current to be lower than minCurrentOpenThr (default 5A) before opening all the contactors simultaneously.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TESTING
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

a. Closing sequence - precharge timeout:
Make sure there are no fault.
Connect a power supply to the link voltage, set the voltage of the power supply to 0V.
Send a connection request to the BMS over CAN.
-> Observe: the negative and the precharge contactors should close.
-> Observe: after Dt_prechgTimeOut seconds, all the contactors should open and the BMS should throw a precharge timeout error on CAN.

b. Closing sequence - Normal:
Make sure there are no fault.
Connect a power supply to the link voltage, set the voltage of the power supply to the same level as the link voltage.
Send a connection request to the BMS over CAN.
-> Observe: the negative and the positive contactors should close, while the precharge is open.

c. Opening sequence - Normal:
Make sure the contactors are closed.
Send a disconnection request to the BMS on CAN.
-> Observe: all the contactors should be open.

d. Critical error - No closing:
Make sure the contactors are open and there are no faults.
Fake a cell over-voltage by changing the maximum critical voltage threshold (from 3.8V to 3.2V for instance). This should trigger a critical limit violation fault.
Send a connection request to the BMS over CAN.
-> Observe: none of the contactors should close and that the BMS stays in error.

e. Critical error - Opening:
Make sure that contactors are closed.
Fake a cell over-voltage by changing the maximum critical voltage threshold (from 3.8V to 3.2V for instance). This should trigger a critical limit violation fault.
-> Observe: all the contactors should open immediately and the BMS should go to error.

f. Opening sequence under load:
Make sure the contactors are closed. Use the power supply to charge or discharge the battery with more than minCurrentOpenThr A (e.g. 6A).
Note: Dont set a too high current as it can damage the contactors!
Send a disconnection request to the BMS on CAN.
-> Observe: all the contactors should open after Dt_loCurrentTimeout (20s) and the BMS should throw a disconnection timeout.



